From: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nl: offload on the outermost device of the chain first

bridger_nl_flow_offload_add() walks the offload chain of the flow's
ingress device and stops at the first device that accepts the flower
filter. For a DSA user port the switch is therefore asked first, and the
conduit - the device whose SoC can actually program the flow - only gets
a chance when the switch reports an error.

On the GL.iNet GL-BE14000 the YT9224 refuses the filter, since it only
offloads ACL rules, but the refusal does not always reach bridger: a
repeated add on the port can come back as a success with no rule
installed, and the flow then stays on the CPU for the rest of its life.
That is what happened on r5 - the first add fell back to eth1 correctly,
a later one reported "Add flow on lan1: Success" while eth1 had no filter
left and the whole transfer was forwarded in software again.

Ask the conduit first and fall back inwards, so a switch that cannot
program the flow - or that answers inaccurately - no longer decides the
outcome. On platforms where the switch does offload the flow, its conduit
is still the SoC that would have taken the rule anyway.

Signed-off-by: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
diff --git a/nl.c b/nl.c
index f326d8e..7b3eeb1 100644
--- a/nl.c
+++ b/nl.c
@@ -876,7 +876,8 @@ __bridger_nl_flow_offload_add(struct bridger_flow *flow, struct device *dev)
 
 int bridger_nl_flow_offload_add(struct bridger_flow *flow)
 {
-	struct device *dev;
+	struct device *devs[4], *dev;
+	unsigned int n_devs = 0;
 	int ifindex;
 	int ret = -1;
 
@@ -886,7 +887,22 @@ int bridger_nl_flow_offload_add(struct bridger_flow *flow)
 	if (flow->offload_ifindex)
 		bridger_nl_flow_offload_del(flow);
 
+	/*
+	 * Offload on the outermost device of the chain first. For a DSA
+	 * user port that is the conduit, which is where the SoC can
+	 * actually program the flow; the switch behind the port usually
+	 * only offloads ACL rules, and a switch that accepts the filter
+	 * without programming it would end the search right here.
+	 */
 	for (dev = flow->fdb_in->dev; dev; dev = dev->offload_dev) {
+		if (n_devs >= ARRAY_SIZE(devs))
+			break;
+
+		devs[n_devs++] = dev;
+	}
+
+	while (n_devs > 0) {
+		dev = devs[--n_devs];
 		ifindex = device_ifindex(dev);
 		ret = __bridger_nl_flow_offload_add(flow, dev);
 		D("Add flow on %s: %s\n", dev->ifname, ret ? strerror(-ret) : "Success");
